// The simulated system: the target a scenario runs, and the channel policies
// that decide what the runner feeds and what it logs.
//
// No `generate` attribute, and that is forced rather than stylistic: the
// grammar admits a `system_target` only in a module that generates nothing. So
// the process, the machine it runs on and the box they host are declared in
// `sim.clk`, and this file names the target over them and emits only the
// process description and per-domain configs the runner reads.
//
// What a scenario needs from this file is the two per-domain configs: the
// channel publisher config that makes two channels come out of an input log --
// the scripts and the injections -- and the event logger config that puts every
// channel worth asserting on into the output log.
//
// The policies live here rather than beside the channels they bind because
// `channel_policy` generates nothing, and a module that generates C++ may not
// import one that generates no targets.
//
// Everything a scenario checker reads is below. `DriverPose` is the machine's
// heartbeat, `DriverCmd` what it was asked for, `SessionCmdChan` what the
// session asked of the driver, `DriverEvt` what the gate did about it,
// `TickFaults` what the decision tick had to say, `ReportsOut` what the session
// said about all of it, `ScheduleChan` what the session made of a script and
// `Estimates` where the head was. The two log-fed channels are logged as well,
// so a run's inputs and its outputs are in one file and a failure can be read
// without the scenario's source in hand.

use cogs::sim;
use cogs::motion;
use @clockwork::clockwork::logging::channel_policy::{ChannelLoggingPolicy,
    LogReaderPolicy, LogType};
use @clockwork::clockwork::logging::channel_type::{ChannelType};

// What makes the runner publish these two channels from the input log. No
// policy carries a `source_name`, so the source is the channel's own name,
// which is what the scenario author writes.
policy LogReaderPolicy for motion::ScriptsIn
{
}

policy LogReaderPolicy for sim::SimCmdChan
{
}

// What the session made of a script: the schedule the mover streams, whole and
// once per change.
policy ChannelLoggingPolicy for motion::ScheduleChan
{
    log_type = LogType::event;
}

// What the run is asked to do, kept beside what it did.
policy ChannelLoggingPolicy for sim::SimCmdChan
{
    log_type = LogType::event;
}

policy ChannelLoggingPolicy for motion::ScriptsIn
{
    log_type = LogType::event;
}

// The machine's heartbeat: one sample per cycle, always.
policy ChannelLoggingPolicy for motion::DriverPose
{
    log_type = LogType::event;
}

// What it was asked to hold next.
policy ChannelLoggingPolicy for motion::DriverCmd
{
    log_type = LogType::event;
}

// What the gate did that the sample stream does not show.
policy ChannelLoggingPolicy for motion::DriverEvt
{
    log_type = LogType::event;
}

// What the session asked the driver for, and what came back: the answer to one
// transaction, and the rotating read of the status registers.
policy ChannelLoggingPolicy for motion::SessionCmdChan
{
    log_type = LogType::event;
}

policy ChannelLoggingPolicy for motion::DriverAuxOut
{
    log_type = LogType::event;
}

policy ChannelLoggingPolicy for motion::DriverHealth
{
    log_type = LogType::event;
}

// What the decision tick raised.
policy ChannelLoggingPolicy for motion::TickFaults
{
    log_type = LogType::event;
}

// What the session said about the session: the whole story so far, republished
// whenever a row is added to it.
//
// Regular, where the two online systems mark it persistent: a deterministic run
// writes its log offboard and records every message from the first, so there is
// no attach instant here for a story to be published in front of.
policy ChannelLoggingPolicy for motion::ReportsOut
{
    log_type = LogType::event;
}

// Everything the driver knows about its own run, republished on a cadence.
//
// Persistent, as the two online systems have it: the newest message is
// cumulative, so a logger handed that one message at attach has the run so far.
// A deterministic run records every message from the first and loses nothing
// either way, and the policy is the same one here so that the record the
// analyzer reads is reached the same way in all three systems.
policy ChannelLoggingPolicy for motion::DriverStatus
{
    log_type = LogType::event;
    channel_type = ChannelType::persistent;
}

// Where the head was, once per sample.
policy ChannelLoggingPolicy for motion::Estimates
{
    log_type = LogType::event;
}

system_target motion_sim
{
    box: sim::System;
}
